        "Kallaroo was rezoned for housing in 1969 and built out through the 1970s around Springfield Primary School and the shops on Bridgewater Drive. That first generation is double brick and brick veneer on strip footings with timber roof framing, now more than forty years old, and it is where most of our termite work in the suburb sits. West of Dampier Avenue the Northshore precinct went up in the mid to late 1980s on younger dune sand, with more slab-on-ground construction and cavity brick. The ground across the suburb is coastal sand over Tamala limestone, which is close to the surface on the ridge and holds moisture in pockets that subterranean termites follow through the dry months.",
        "The Whitfords Nodes foreshore reserve and the coastal dune between the shared path and the beach are the suburb's main wildlife corridor. Rats move off the dunes into roof spaces along the ocean-side streets as the weather cools, and redbacks and black house spiders live in the limestone coping and retic pits that line almost every front garden here. The street names remember the Batavia and other wrecks; the salt air that sank them also corrodes flashings and mesh, so proofing advice in Kallaroo leans to stainless and aluminium. Bridgewater Park and the school ovals keep coastal brown ant colonies well fed under adjacent paving.",
